// PARAM: --analysis interval
// The concrete value of total here is fixed at 7, so both checks genuinely
// hold -- and the context-insensitive analysis establishes neither.
//
// Mechanism: the loss is on total, not on the entered formal. inc's body
// writes total := total + n, so total's own value re-enters its next
// contribution and the chain 0, [0,3], [0,7], ... ascends without bound;
// Apinis warrowing must cut the upper bound to terminate. n itself is exact
// at [3,4].
//
// The cut is where the kind enters. +inf is not an int32, so the next
// total + n reaches the conversion's give-up branch and answers the whole
// int32 range, taking the lower bound with it -- which is why 0 < total is
// undecided here and not merely total < 100. Saturating the widening at
// int32's own maximum, as Goblint's interval widening does, would not
// recover it either: [0,2147483647] + [3,4] overflows just the same, and the
// wrapped result has no single-interval representation but the whole range.
// Under sem.int.signed_overflow=assume_none the addition would keep
// [3,2147483651] and the lower bound would survive; VIMP freezes wraparound
// instead, so the whole range is the correct answer.
//
// The chain has a single write origin, so the per-origin update rules do
// not recover it either: they separate contributions from distinct origins
// before widening, and there is only one origin here to separate.
//
// Context sensitivity does: under --context entry-state the two call sites
// keep separate entry states, nothing feeds back into itself, and both
// checks are decided. 03-global_across_calls_entry_state.vimp is that half.

global int32 total;
void inc(int32 n) {
  total := total + n
}
void main() {
  total := 0;
  inc(3);
  inc(4);
  __voblint_check(0 < total);   // UNKNOWN
  __voblint_check(total < 100)  // UNKNOWN
}
